WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.431
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.431 --> 00:00:03.120
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.820
My name is Karthi Thyagarajan.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.420
I'm a solutions architect
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.270
So I'm going to talk about
Cloud MemoryStore for Redis,

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.130
as well as OpenCensus today.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.560
First, Cloud
MemoryStore for Redis.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.250
It provides a fully managed
in-memory Datastore service

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.730
that's built on scalable,
secure, and highly

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.640
available infrastructure
that's managed by Google.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.040
Now, caching capability,
as most of you know,

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.960
is extremely important for
most production applications,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.240
and latency goes
hand-in-hand with caching.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.580
The way most people reduce
latency is using caching.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.080
Now, tracing this latency
in an end-to-end fashion

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.260
for your application is
the role of observability.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.770
And it helps you design
your application faster,

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.440
and it will also help you
diagnose issues faster.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.960
Now, I should point out that
Cloud MemoryStore for Redis

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.050
already has powerful monitoring
capabilities built in,

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.330
but this is entirely
on the server side.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.190
In this video,
what we'll look at

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.830
is how you can build
observability of attributes,

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.020
like end-to-end
latency, directly

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.100
into your applications with
something called OpenCensus.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.710
OpenCensus is a
vendor agnostic set

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.680
of libraries that
provides metric collection

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.830
as well as tracing capabilities.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.840
So before we get
to our demo, let's

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.160
look at the application
architecture.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.980
Our application is a simple
three-tier application,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.980
and it's hosted on
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.710
At its core, it consists of
a simple Java Spring Boot

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.233
application, and users interact
with this application via GCPs,

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:44.410
https Load Balancer.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.560
And for the database
here, our application

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.630
uses Cloud SQL, specifically
the MySQL engine in Cloud SQL.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.300
When the user issues a query
against our application,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.280
the results returned
from the database

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.570
are cached using Cloud
MemoryStore for Redis.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.229
With that context, let's
see the demo in action.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:04.770
So in this application,
what we'll do

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.100
is we will look for our
employees in our directory

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.120
by typing in part of their name.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.300
In this case, we'll
type in Mel, and we

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.370
see that a whole bunch
of results are returned.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.080
In addition to those
results, we also

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.260
see that the application tells
us how long that query took,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.360
248 milliseconds in this case.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.130
Now, let's try that query again.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.730
Type in Mel again, and
as you can see now,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.230
it only took one millisecond.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.060
That's because the
results were cached

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.780
in Cloud MemoryStore for Redis.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.480
Let's dive a little
deeper into it

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.780
by looking at what
OpenCensus tracing tells us.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.990
We've configured OpenCensus
to perform trace logging

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.560
into Cloud Trace, which is a
service offered by Google Cloud

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.610
Platform.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.440
Navigating to
Cloud Trace, we can

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.580
see that our trace logs are
easily identifiable by the name

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:53.960
of the function that we used.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.850
And in this case,
it also tells us

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:59.540
how long the overall function
took, 248 milliseconds

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.100
approximately.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.250
OpenCensus tracing also
lets us dive deeper

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.340
into our overall function call.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.780
You can see here that our
application attempted to get

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.460
the results from the cache--

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.380
in this case, Cloud
MemoryStore for Redis

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.910
did not find it in the cache,
hence the request to MySQL.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:21.320
Down below, you can see that
MySQL took approximately 244

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.440
milliseconds, about twice
the order of magnitude

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.010
that it would take to
fetch the data from Redis.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.630
We can also see
here that we have

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.630
annotated cache misses
to help us better trace

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.160
our application.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.550
Diving a little deeper into what
happens after our results are

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.220
fetched from MySQL, you can
see that our application

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.400
added the results to the
cache using addToCache.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.715
What about the second
time the query is issued?

00:03:46.715 --> 00:03:48.840
We expect the results to
be fetched from the cache,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.630
and that's what we see
using OpenCensus tracing.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.940
We can see that this query
took a lot less time,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.530
around one millisecond.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.320
Because the results
were found in the cache,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.760
it did not have to
access the database,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.660
and we saved approximately
240 milliseconds.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.910
That's quite a bit of time.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.460
So that was the happy path.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.230
You can see how we also have
library level tracing here.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.330
Now, let's see how
OpenCensus can help

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.820
us diagnose issues that occur.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.579
What we'll do here is we'll
simulate a network issue where

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.550
we cannot reach our Redis host.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.970
And we'll type in
the name Mel again.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.650
While we expect this to take a
lot less time because we expect

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:29.066
the results to be
in the cache, we

00:04:29.066 --> 00:04:32.690
see that it actually takes
240 milliseconds or so,

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.980
so there's something wrong
with our application.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.990
There's too much latency.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.870
So let's go back to
OpenCensus tracing

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.090
and look for the
most recent request.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:47.190
Diving deeper into it, we see
that there was a cache miss,

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.380
and the reason
for the cache miss

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.720
is that our application was
unable to connect to Redis.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.047
Now, in production, we would
have to chase this error down,

00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:58.880
and it would be a lot
more difficult to find

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.860
the error without this
tracing capability.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.530
Now, we know that it's a
network issue or some kind

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.710
of connectivity issue getting
to Cloud MemoryStore for Redis,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.570
and we know where to
focus our efforts.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:12.206
So that can save us a lot
of time during diagnosis.

00:05:12.206 --> 00:05:13.580
Hopefully, that
gives you an idea

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.340
of how you can use OpenCensus
for both improving performance

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.090
as well as diagnosing issues.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.040
To summarize,
we've looked at how

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.980
you can use Cloud
MemoryStore for Redis

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.100
to significantly reduce
latency in your application.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.690
Following that, we
also saw how you

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.750
can build in end-to-end
observability

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.200
directly in your application
using OpenCensus.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.380
If you're interested in using
OpenCensus in your application,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.260
please visit opencensus.io.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.830
So thank you for watching.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.430
If you enjoyed this video,
please click the Like button

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:46.990
and subscribe to this channel.

